# Command trivy

## What does this command do:

Find vulnerabilities, misconfigurations, secrets, SBOM in containers, Kubernetes, code repositories, clouds, vm's and more

## Installed from

For Fedora install using:
```
sudo tee /etc/yum.repos.d/trivy.repo << 'EOF'
[trivy]
name=Trivy repository
baseurl=https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy-repo/rpm/releases/$basearch/
gpgcheck=1
enabled=1
gpgkey=https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy-repo/rpm/public.key
EOF
sudo dnf install -y trivy
```

For Raspberry Pi install using:
```
sudo apt-get install -y wget gnupg
wget -qO - https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy-repo/deb/public.key | gpg --dearmor | sudo tee /usr/share/keyrings/trivy.gpg > /dev/null
echo "deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/trivy.gpg] https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy-repo/deb generic main" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/trivy.list
sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y trivy
```

For Mac install using: `brew install trivy`

For Mac container (Fedora Linux, ARM/aarch64) install using:
```
sudo tee /etc/yum.repos.d/trivy.repo << 'EOF'
[trivy]
name=Trivy repository
baseurl=https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy-repo/rpm/releases/$basearch/
gpgcheck=1
enabled=1
gpgkey=https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy-repo/rpm/public.key
EOF
sudo dnf install -y trivy
```

## Command path

For Fedora run: /usr/bin/trivy
For Raspberry Pi run: /usr/bin/trivy
For Mac run: /opt/homebrew/bin/trivy
For Mac container run: /usr/bin/trivy

## Command documentation

Docs can be found at https://github.com/aquasecurity/trivy

## Command options

```sh
# Scan a container image
trivy image python:3.4-alpine

# Scan a container image from a tar archive
trivy image --input ruby-3.1.tar

# Scan local filesystem
trivy fs .

# Scan a git repository
trivy repository https://github.com/aquasecurity/trivy

# Scan for misconfigurations (IaC, Dockerfile, k8s manifests)
trivy config ./path/to/iac

# Scan a Kubernetes cluster
trivy kubernetes --report summary cluster

# Scan a virtual machine image
trivy vm /path/to/vm.vmdk

# Generate an SBOM
trivy image --format cyclonedx --output sbom.json python:3.4-alpine

# Scan an SBOM for vulnerabilities
trivy sbom sbom.json

# Output as JSON
trivy image -f json -o results.json python:3.4-alpine

# Output as SARIF (for GitHub code scanning)
trivy image -f sarif -o results.sarif python:3.4-alpine

# Filter by severity
trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL python:3.4-alpine

# Ignore unfixed vulnerabilities
trivy image --ignore-unfixed python:3.4-alpine

# Run in server mode
trivy server --listen 0.0.0.0:4954

# Use a remote trivy server
trivy image --server http://localhost:4954 python:3.4-alpine

# Clear cache
trivy clean --all

# Generate shell completion
trivy completion bash | sudo tee /etc/bash_completion.d/trivy
```

### Global flags

```
-c, --config string      config path (default "trivy.yaml")
-d, --debug              debug mode
-f, --format string      version format (json)
-q, --quiet              suppress progress bar and log output
    --timeout duration   timeout (default 5m0s)
-v, --version            show version
    --cache-dir string   cache directory (default "~/.cache/trivy")
    --insecure           allow insecure server connections
```
